{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"GATE FAIL\n\n1. **The rendered request tells the model to return a field the schema rejects.** Both earlier-answer blocks retain judging-v7’s instruction that `cites_views` must be empty. The v13 schema forbids the field entirely. I added `cites_views: []` to the otherwise passing rendered answer and reproduced the schema refusal. **Fix:** strip the old output instructions from the earlier-answer blocks and leave one v13 instruction after the evidence. [Renderer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:87), [Rendered request](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render-r1.request.json)\n\n2. **The rendered answer can bypass the prior-reference check.** I put the authenticated `$414B` prior figure in `reference.figure` and supplied an `unavailable` reason alongside it. Both the schema and checker accepted the answer. C5 takes the `unavailable` branch and skips the figure. **Fix:** require exactly one non-null reference choice and add this case alongside P3a. [Reference schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:65), [C5 dispatch](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:342)\n\n3. **G2 passes by skipping v7’s paired-scenario rule.** The fixture declares no shared quantity and uses one range where v7 calls for paired point scenarios. I reconstructed q02 with matching low and high paths from q01. C4 rejected it because, while checking the high path, it also walks the low path and calls it a bypass. **Fix:** run C4 within each scenario, then check the final range against those paired paths. Make G2 exercise that representation and retain R1b as the bypass control. [Scenario design](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:61), [G2 fixture](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:222), [C4 checker](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:377)"}}
